<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Basco y Vargas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1733">1733</a>–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Basco y Vargas was a Spanish naval officer who served as governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1778 to 1787. He sought to make the colony pay for itself, founding the Economic Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Country in 1781 and introducing a government tobacco monopoly in 1782. The monopoly brought large revenues but was deeply resented by Filipino farmers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-basco-y-vargas.jpg" alt="José Basco y Vargas" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Basco_y_Vargas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Basco was an officer of the Spanish Navy when King Charles III named him governor-general after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-de-anda-y-salazar">Simón de Anda y Salazar</a> in October 1776, though he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> only in July 1778. Officials of the Real Audiencia soon conspired to have his appointment revoked and to put Sarrio in his place, but Sarrio refused and told Basco of the plot, and the conspirators were arrested and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Until then the colony depended on a yearly Mexican subsidy. Basco's Plan general economico aimed to free it from that subsidy and from the Chinese trade. His economic society rewarded the planting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a>, mining and manufacture, and in Camarines he urged the planting of at least four million mulberry trees. From 1782 growers in tobacco provinces such as Cagayan could sell only to the government at fixed prices, and tobacco became the colony's first real internal revenue. In 1785 the Royal Company of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> received a monopoly of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the islands.</p><p>Against Moro raids he strengthened the forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, Cavite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a> and sent an expedition that drove the raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindoro">Mindoro</a>. In 1782 he sent an expedition to Batanes, partly to keep the islands from the British, and on 26 June 1783 Ivatan representatives performed the rites of annexation; the capital was named Basco after him. The Ivatans revolted from 1785, as did the Igorots of Nueva Vizcaya under Lagutao. He also repressed highwaymen near Manila and in 1778 revoked the order expelling the Chinese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Despite his efforts Basco remained disliked and opposed. His first offer to resign was refused, but the king later allowed him to hand the government to Pedro Sarrio, and he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at the end of November 1787. He was promoted in the navy and made governor of Cartagena. The economic society he founded, though often inactive, remained important to the colony's development for a full century after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Basco y Vargas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor <b>Jose Basco y Vargas</b> introduced the tobacco monopoly in 1782.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Málaga, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.74,-5.17&amp;year=1779" aria-label="Ronda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4420 840 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José Basco y Vargas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4870 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4870 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.74,-5.17&amp;year=1779">What was here in 1779? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
